se model so that's nice if  you're really trying to save money but  these two machines the 14 and 16 already  come with 512 which is a pretty decent  starting point and of course you can  upgrade to one terabyte on these  machines which is worth it if you're  already spending that much money now in  terms of the ram these machines come  with eight gigs standard while the 14  and 16 come with 16 gigs which i would  say is a decent area to stay at unless  you're doing a lot of productivity work  then you might want to upgrade but in  terms of these if you're doing a lot of  productivity work and multitasking i  would definitely recommend going up to  16 gigs of ram however that creates an  issue because with the m2 air if you  upgrade to the 512 gig ssd and then you  also upgrade to 16 gigs of ram you're  looking at 1600  while of course this 14 inch macbook pro  you can get on sale for 1800 and it has  a ton of advantages over the m2 macbook  air so i would say if you really care  enough about productivity to upgrade the  ram on the m2 you might as well just be  looking at these higher end machines so  with that said let me get into my  buyer's guide portion where i recommend  who should buy each one of these  macbooks now starting with the m1  macbook air this is perfect for the  person who's just trying to get into  apple laptops and wants to spend a  little bit of money because you can buy  it for 850 dollars which is a killer  deal basically destroying all other  windows laptops so if you have light  usage or some light business use this is  great now as far as the m2 macbook air i  think this one is for those people who  really want the fresh redesign who like  the new design who like the notch the  better display that is for those people  that are willing to spend the money on  the extra design now in terms of the m2  macbook pro  that's for the people who don't want to  go up to the 14 inch they absolutely  can't spend 1800 but they do want a fan  for extended